New York will soon ban the sale of children's toys with a built-in AI companion, as the law defines it. On 24 November 2026, Governor Kathy Hochul signed bill S.9408A/A.11144B, a temporary ban on what the statute calls chatbot toys. Her office gives plush animals, dolls and robots that go online to chat with kids as examples of the target. The timing matters for shoppers: the ban is not yet in force.

*Disclosure: this post was drafted by Claude, an AI assistant made by Anthropic, and checked by a separate AI reviewer against the cited pages for figures, dates and wording. This is not a full human review. Stackbrook did not test any toy and has no affiliate relationship with any company mentioned at the time of writing. The header image is AI-generated. This is general information, not legal advice.*

## What the law prohibits

The amended bill adds a short new Article 48 to New York's General Business Law. Its core sentence says that no person or business may manufacture, exchange, hold for sale, distribute or sell any chatbot toy in the state. The wording also reaches agents, employees, affiliates, beneficial owners and subsidiaries.

A chatbot toy is defined as an AI companion embedded in or integrated with a children's toy. "Children's toy" is borrowed from federal law (15 U.S.C. § 2057c(g)), which we did not open for this article.

The text of the amended bill contains no list of exceptions. The limits come mainly from how "AI companion" is defined.

## What counts as an AI companion

The bill points to an existing definition in section 1700 of the General Business Law. As written there, an AI companion is a system using artificial intelligence, generative AI and/or emotion recognition that is "designed to simulate a sustained human or human-like relationship with a user". It does so by:

1. retaining information from earlier sessions and the user's preferences to personalise the interaction and keep the user engaged;
2. asking unprompted, emotion-based questions that go beyond answering what the user said; and
3. keeping up an ongoing dialogue about matters personal to the user.

The three items are joined by "and". Our reading is that a system needs all of them, but we found no official guidance that says how this will be applied to toys. A toy that only plays recorded phrases would appear to fall outside the wording, though that is our interpretation and not a ruling.

The statutory definition as we read it does not mention an internet connection, so do not rule a toy out on that basis alone.

The same section excludes business systems used solely for customer service or strictly to give information about the business's own products, services and customer accounts, systems primarily designed and marketed for efficiency, research or technical assistance, and systems a business uses solely internally or for employee productivity.

![Flow diagram of the questions the New York definition asks, from whether a product is a children's toy to whether its AI has all three companion features](/images/site1/new-york-ai-chatbot-toy-ban-explained-chart.svg)
*Chart created by Stackbrook from the cited pages. It simplifies the statute and leaves out its exclusions.*

## When it starts and how long it lasts

The act takes effect on the ninetieth day after becoming law, and the governor's office says it will not be in effect this holiday season. Counting 90 days from the 24 November signature gives about 22 February 2027. That date is our calculation, not one printed on the pages we read, so check the official chapter text if the exact day matters to you.

The law also has a built-in end. It expires and is repealed five years after its effective date, which by the same arithmetic would be in early 2032 unless lawmakers change it. The sponsor's memo calls it a temporary moratorium.

Lawmakers passed it in June. The Senate vote on 1 June 2026 was 57 in favour and 3 against, with the others absent or excused, and the Assembly passed it the next day. It is recorded as chapter 416 of 2026.

## Penalties and enforcement

Enforcement sits with the New York Attorney General, who may go to court to stop an "operator" (explained below) and may seek civil penalties of up to $15,000 per day for each violation. For breaches of the sales ban, the state does not have to prove that anyone was injured.

One drafting detail is worth noting. The ban applies to any person or business, while the enforcement paragraph speaks of an "operator", defined as whoever operates or provides an AI companion in a children's toy. How that applies to a shop that merely stocks such a product is a question for a lawyer, and we have seen no official answer.

## What retailers and sellers can do now

For a small business that sells toys into New York, a few practical steps follow from the text:

- **List conversational toys.** Note which products in your range hold open-ended conversations with a child.
- **Ask suppliers in writing.** Does the toy remember past chats, ask emotional questions unprompted, or carry on personal conversations? Those mirror the three statutory features, but not the further test that the AI is designed to simulate a relationship. Treat the questions as a starting point, not a compliance test, and get advice before relying on a supplier's answers.
- **Plan stock around February 2027.** The ban covers holding for sale and distributing, not only the final sale.
- **Get advice on online orders.** The bill refers to selling "in this state". We did not find official guidance on how that treats particular online or cross-border sales.

## What parents can check this season

Because the ban is not yet active, a toy on a New York shelf or website this December is not removed by this law. Before buying any talking toy, it is reasonable to read the product page and privacy notice for whether it connects to the internet, whether it keeps conversation history, who receives recordings and how to delete them. If you cannot find clear answers, you can ask the seller or choose a different gift.

## The wider picture

New York's governor signed separate laws on 28 October 2026 on unsafe AI chatbot features for minors and on internet-connected baby monitors, and the 24 November release refers back to them. At federal level, the Federal Trade Commission opened an inquiry on 11 September 2025, sending information orders to seven companies, including Alphabet, Meta, OpenAI, Snap, xAI and Character Technologies, about AI chatbots that act as companions, including effects on children. That release is about chatbots and does not mention toys.

## What we could not confirm

We did not find official guidance listing specific products that are covered, and we did not read the Assembly's tally or the federal toy definition. We also did not read the governor's approval memo, listed as memo 14, or check for later amendments, either of which could affect dates or scope. Rules in other states or countries are outside this article.
